<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Artaxata</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Artashat</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Artaxata was the capital of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artaxias-i">Artaxias I</a> in the early 2nd century BCE on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aras-river">Araxes</a> river, near the modern town of Artashat. Ancient writers say <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> advised on its site. It remained the main royal city for centuries, was taken and burned by the Roman general Corbulo in 58 CE and later rebuilt, before giving way as capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vagharshapat">Vagharshapat</a>. Its remains lie near the monastery of Khor Virap.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/artaxata.jpg" alt="Artaxata" width="320" height="222" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vyacheslav Argenberg · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mount_Ararat_and_Khor_Virap_Monastery_2,_Ararat_Plain,_Armenia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
